.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd September 30, 2026
.Dt DEVFS 4
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm devfs
.Nd device file system
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
file system provides
.Pa /dev .
It is mounted at boot, before any process runs, and made afresh at
every boot; nothing in it is written to disk.
The
.Pa /dev
directory on the disk is only its mountpoint, and whatever an older
installation left there is hidden.
.Pp
The kernel keeps a registry of character devices: each has a name, a
major and minor number, an owner, a group, a mode, and a driver that
opens it.
Drivers in the kernel and in kernel modules add their devices to the
registry when the devices exist; there is no interface for user programs.
.Nm
holds a node for every registered device, with the owner, group and mode
the registry gives it, and a directory
.Pa shm ,
mode 01777, where
.Xr shm_open 3
and
.Xr sem_open 3
keep POSIX shared memory objects and named semaphores.
When a path lookup enters
.Pa /dev
and the registry has changed since, nodes are added for new devices and
removed for devices no longer registered before the lookup continues.
.Pp
Changes to
.Nm
last until reboot:
.Bl -bullet
.It
.Xr unlink 2
of a device node hides it: it does not come back when the registry
changes, only at the next boot.
.It
.Xr chmod 2
and
.Xr chown 2
of a node last until reboot;
.Xr devfs.conf 5
makes such changes permanent.
.It
.Xr mknod 2
of a device in
.Nm
fails with
.Er EPERM :
devices come from drivers.
.It
Other files can be created and last until reboot: sockets, such as the
.Pa /dev/log
.Xr syslogd 8
binds, symbolic links, directories, FIFOs, and ordinary files under
.Pa /dev/shm .
.El
.Pp
Opening a device node goes through the registry by its number, wherever
the node is:
.Xr mknod 2
outside
.Pa /dev
still makes device nodes on the disk, and they open the registered
device with that number, or fail with
.Er ENXIO
if there is none.
.Pp
.Pa /proc/mounts
lists
.Nm
as
.Dq Li devfs /dev devfs .
.Ss Devices
.Bl -column "urandom" "major, minor" "root:tty" "Mode" -offset indent
.It Sy Name Ta Sy Number Ta Sy Owner Ta Sy Mode
.It Li null Ta 1, 3 Ta root Ta 0666
.It Li zero Ta 1, 5 Ta root Ta 0666
.It Li random Ta 1, 8 Ta root Ta 0666
.It Li urandom Ta 1, 9 Ta root Ta 0666
.It Li ttyv0 Ta 4, 0 Ta root:tty Ta 0600
.It Li tty Ta 5, 0 Ta root Ta 0666
.It Li console Ta 5, 1 Ta root Ta 0600
.It Li klog Ta 7, 0 Ta root Ta 0600
.It Li fb0 Ta 29, 0 Ta root Ta 0666
.El
.Pp
.Pa /dev/fb0
exists only when the system has a framebuffer.
.Pa /dev/tty
reaches only the opener's own controlling terminal.
.Xr login 1
gives a terminal to the user who logs in on it.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /etc/devfs.conf -compact
.It Pa /dev
The mountpoint.
.It Pa /etc/devfs.conf
Owners, modes and links applied at boot.
.El
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er EPERM
.Xr mknod 2
of a device in
.Nm .
.It Bq Er ENXIO
A device node's number has no registered device.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr mknod 2 ,
.Xr klog 4 ,
.Xr devfs.conf 5 ,
.Xr rc 8
.Sh HISTORY
OxideBSD's
.Nm
appeared in 2026, modelled on
.Fx Ns 's .
Before it, the nodes in
.Pa /dev
were files on the disk, made once when it was formatted, so a node
removed with
.Xr rm 1
stayed gone across reboots.
